import IO

/-!
# Ref

Mutable cells owned by linear points-to tokens (`import Ref`; plan CS7b/S4, cohesion
step M5, /workspace/wiz-notes/cohesion-resources.md; docs/resources.md).

A location `l : Loc V` is an ordinary (ω) value: it can be copied, stored in data, and
sent anywhere. Owning the cell is a separate value, the token `Pts l v`: "`l` holds
`v`, and I am the only one who may read or write it". Tokens are used at quantity 1,
which is exact in Wiz, so

 * a token can't be copied: one owner per cell (no aliasing of mutable state);
 * a token can't be dropped: every cell is freed exactly once (`Ref.free`), no leaks,
   no double free;
 * reading or writing needs the token: no use after free, and a function can't touch a
   cell it isn't given (the frame rule, by typing).

The token's second index is the cell's current value, as a ghost (erased) index, so a
function's type is its Hoare triple: `Ref.set l w : (1 t : Pts l v) → … Pts l w …` is
`{l ↦ v} set l w {l ↦ w}` (lib/Hoare.wiz, `Triple.write`), and `Ref.get` returns the
value with an erased proof that it is the token's index (`Triple.read`).

**What is trusted.** The four natives `wiz_ref_*` (runtime/, over the MVar
cells of 25d) and the types given to them below; the kernel checks everything else.
The one type that asserts a fact is `Ref.readRaw`'s: the value read equals the token's
index. It holds by an invariant the API keeps: a cell is created with its token
(`Ref.new`, index = the value stored), the only write (`Ref.set`) consumes the token and
returns one indexed by the value written, nothing else reaches the cell (`Loc` and
`Pts` are opaque, their natives private), and a token can't be duplicated. So the
value in the cell is always the index of its one token. This is the meaning of the
token in lib/HProp.wiz's model, `Pts l v` ↔ `l ↦ v`; a mechanised adequacy proof
against `Prog.run` (lib/Hoare.wiz) is future work. `#print axioms` lists the natives.
Extern privacy is by name only: another module could bind `wiz_ref_new` with any type
(it would show in `#print axioms`), the same gap as MVar.Hole, Lock.Guard and Chan.

Tokens are boxed units at run time (a zero-size representation is plan L5). In
compiled code only: the interpreters (`#eval`, `#run`) don't run these natives.
-/

/-- A cell's location: an ordinary (ω) value, which can be copied, stored and sent. -/
axiom Loc : Type → Type
-- owning the cell `l`, which holds `v`: no constructor, no ω producer
/-- Owning the cell `l`, which holds `v`: no constructor and no ω producer, so a token is
used at quantity 1 (exactly once). -/
axiom Pts : {V : Type} → Loc V → V → Type

-- a full cell and its token
private extern "wiz_ref_new" Ref.newRaw : {0 V : Type} → (v : V) → IO ((l : Loc V) × Pts l v)
-- the value, which is the token's index (the trusted fact), and the token back
private extern "wiz_ref_read" Ref.readRaw : {0 V : Type} → (l : Loc V) → {0 v : V} → (1 t : Pts l v) →
  IO ((a : V) × (a = v) × Pts l v)
-- write `w`; the token now says so
private extern "wiz_ref_write" Ref.writeRaw : {0 V : Type} → (l : Loc V) → (w : V) → {0 v : V} → (1 t : Pts l v) →
  IO (Pts l w)
-- give up the cell: the token is consumed and the value released (the cell's memory goes
-- when the last `Loc` does)
private extern "wiz_ref_free" Ref.freeRaw : {0 V : Type} → (l : Loc V) → {0 v : V} → (1 t : Pts l v) → IO Unit

-- `{emp} new v {l. l ↦ v}`: a new cell holding `v`, and its token
/-- A new cell holding `v`, and its token (`{emp} new v {l. l ↦ v}`). -/
def Ref.new {V B : Type} (v : V) (1 k : (l : Loc V) → (1 t : Pts l v) → IO B) : IO B :=
  fun w => let 1 (r, w') := Ref.newRaw v w; k r.1 r.2 w'
-- `{l ↦ v} get l {a. \[a = v] ⋆ l ↦ v}`: reading takes the token and hands it back (an
-- erased use would not do: a stale token, kept at quantity 0 past a `set`, would read the
-- new value with a proof that it is the old one)
/-- The value, with an erased proof that it is the token's index, and the token back
(`{l ↦ v} get l {a. [a = v] ⋆ l ↦ v}`). Reading takes the token: an erased use would
let a stale token, kept past a `set`, read the new value with a proof that it is the old
one. -/
def Ref.get {V B : Type} (l : Loc V) {0 v : V} (1 t : Pts l v)
    (1 k : (a : V) → (0 e : a = v) → (1 t : Pts l v) → IO B) : IO B :=
  fun w => let 1 (r, w') := Ref.readRaw l t w; k r.1 r.2.1 r.2.2 w'
/-- Write `w`; the token comes back indexed by `w` (`{l ↦ v} set l x {l ↦ x}`). -/
def Ref.set {V B : Type} (l : Loc V) (x : V) {0 v : V} (1 t : Pts l v) (1 k : (1 t' : Pts l x) → IO B) : IO B :=
  fun w => let 1 (t', w') := Ref.writeRaw l x t w; k t' w'
/-- Give up the cell: the token is consumed and the value released (`{l ↦ v} free l
{emp}`); the cell's memory goes when the last `Loc` does. -/
def Ref.free {V B : Type} (l : Loc V) {0 v : V} (1 t : Pts l v) (1 next : IO B) : IO B :=
  fun w => let 1 (u, w') := Ref.freeRaw l t w; next w'

/-- A token whose index is known equal to another value, retyped (erased proof, linear
token). -/
def Pts.cast {V : Type} {l : Loc V} {0 a b : V} (0 e : a = b) (1 t : Pts l a) : Pts l b :=
  Eq.subst (Pts l) e t
